## 2. Literature Review

### 2.1 Fake News Detection Approaches

Research in automated fake news detection has explored various methodological approaches, broadly categorized into content-based, social context-based, and hybrid methods. Content-based approaches, which form the foundation of this project, analyze linguistic features, writing style, and textual characteristics to identify patterns associated with false information.

Studies have demonstrated that fake news articles often exhibit distinct linguistic patterns, including increased use of emotional language, sensationalist terms, and less formal writing structures compared to legitimate news sources. These differences provide the basis for machine learning classification approaches.

### 2.2 Natural Language Processing in Text Classification

The application of NLP techniques in text classification has proven effective across various domains, with particular success in sentiment analysis, spam detection, and document categorization. Feature extraction methods such as Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency (TF-IDF) have shown robust performance in capturing important textual patterns while maintaining computational efficiency.

TF-IDF vectorization transforms textual data into numerical representations that machine learning algorithms can process effectively. This technique emphasizes words that are frequent in specific documents but rare across the entire corpus, thereby highlighting distinctive vocabulary patterns.

### 2.3 Machine Learning Algorithms for Text Classification

Various machine learning algorithms have been employed for fake news detection, including Support Vector Machines (SVM), Naive Bayes, Random Forest, and Logistic Regression. Logistic Regression has demonstrated particular effectiveness in text classification tasks due to its interpretability, computational efficiency, and strong performance on high-dimensional sparse data typical of text features.

The linear nature of Logistic Regression allows for clear interpretation of feature importance, enabling understanding of which linguistic patterns contribute most significantly to classification decisions. This interpretability is crucial for building trust and understanding in automated fake news detection systems.

